The problem. Live: task 23 was cancelled (`spawn task cancel`) while its resident was in the middle of a turn. Nothing told the worker. It kept working on the cancelled objective for 45 more minutes. `spawn_task_cancel` only records `task.cancelled`, and the dispatcher does nothing for a resident whose task is cancelled:
- `DELIVERY_TASKS` does not even return a resident's cancelled task;
- `orders()` stops a finished ephemeral worker and a retired resident, nobody else;
- the next task is typed in as soon as the worker is idle, into the same workspace.
The work done for the cancelled task stays: the Bridge's checkpoints commit it to the agent's branch, the pusher publishes it to `spawn/<agent>`, and the next task starts on top of it. In a gated mission the next task's candidate is that branch, so the cancelled work is judged and landed under another objective's name, unseen. Its report, if it writes one, is not collected (the task is no longer delivered), but nothing else stops. The same holds for every other way a delivered task closes without landing:
- an integrator abort over budget (`task.aborted`), which stops the worker but leaves the work for the resident's next task;
- a gate rejection the closure rule turns into a cancel;
- a mission closed or dropped under a working resident.

Required behaviour (the contract the gate checks). The property is at the source: a closed task's live work ends with it.
1. Promptly stopped. When a delivered task is cancelled, whatever the cause, and its worker's latest delivery was that task, the worker stops working on it within 15 s, and its turn for the task is not left to finish. The causes: `spawn task cancel`, the supervisor, an integrator abort, or the closure rule. An engine in the middle of a turn is interrupted (Claude Code interrupts on Escape; `send_keys` exists) or its execution is stopped (a Stop takes a final checkpoint first). Either is fine. A worker that is idle at its prompt is not interrupted.
2. Told. A resident (any worker that will take more work in that session) is told within 30 s of the cancel that task #N was cancelled. The message names the task and says it is cancelled. It reaches the worker typed in at its prompt, or as the prompt of a start or resume, before anything else is delivered to it. No later task is typed into a worker that has not been told. An ephemeral worker is stopped, as today, and is not told. A retired resident is stopped, as today, and is not resumed only to be told.
3. Never collected. No report for a cancelled task is collected, recorded (`task.reported`) or judged (`task.gated`). That includes a report the worker writes after the cancel, and a report file it leaves in the workspace: the next task's report is collected only if it was written for that task (the delivery mark rule, unchanged).
4. Set aside, not carried over. Before the worker gets anything else, its workspace and branch return to the state the cancelled task started from: the agent's checkpoint its delivery was made on, i.e. its latest `artifact.committed` before the task's `task.delivered`. For a task typed into a live worker, that includes the main merge done just before the delivery. Do it as a new commit on top of the branch's head, after the worker's final checkpoint (which keeps what it did). The new commit's tree is that start checkpoint's tree. Record it as a checkpoint of the agent (`artifact.committed`) and publish it like any checkpoint. The notice of 2 says that the work was set aside and where it is (the commit that holds it). Why this and not the alternatives:
   - Not a rewind: pushes are never forced (push.rs), and the integrator requires the pushed head to descend from the agent's latest checkpoint (`freshness()`).
   - Nothing is destroyed: a person can recover the work from the branch's history.
   - The next task's candidate holds none of it in its tree, so nothing rides into another objective's landing.
   - Not "the next task's brief says so": that relies on a model undoing its own work exactly, nobody checks it, and the gate would still judge a candidate that holds both.
   - Not a reset to main: in a mission accepted by review, accepted work never lands, so main lacks the resident's earlier accepted work. In a gated mission, the start checkpoint already is the last landed state, merged with main.
   It holds whether the worker was in the middle of a turn, idle (a cancel after the report), or not running at all (stopped, lost). The branch moves only while nothing of the agent writes it: after the stop, or between turns under the branch's lock, as `merge_base` does. Never under a running turn, and never past a final checkpoint that has not been taken.
5. Nothing else moves. These stay as they are:
   - a task never delivered (cancelled while assigned) changes nothing for its worker;
   - a resident whose cancelled task is not its latest delivery (a later task was delivered since) is left alone;
   - retirement, the stop of finished ephemeral workers, and accepted, landing and landed work.
   The dispatcher stays stateless: idempotency keys and the log say what was already done, and every pass after the first sends nothing new.
6. Say it:
   - in the module comment of deliver.rs (the bullet "an ephemeral worker whose task was accepted or cancelled is stopped");
   - in docs/fleet-architecture.md, where tasks close and in the closure rule;
   - in docs/storage.md (the set-aside checkpoint, next to the main merge);
   - in the usage text of `spawn task cancel`.
